        id: 'khayamiya-restoration',
        slug: "khayamiya-market-restoration-begins",
        title: "Cairo's Governor launches restoration of the historic Khayamiya Market",
        excerpt: "The first phase of restoring Cairo's historic tentmakers' market, Souq Al-Khayamiya, has begun — an 8-month, 100-million-pound project led by the Aga Khan Cultural Services – Egypt and ALIPH.",
        content:
          "Dr Ibrahim Saber, Governor of Cairo, inspected the start of the first phase of the restoration and rehabilitation of the historic Khayamiya Market at Qasabat Radwan Bey in the Central Cairo district. The work supports the commercial and craft activity already under way inside the market, as part of wider efforts to preserve the architectural heritage and traditional crafts of historic Cairo.\n\nThe project is jointly funded by the Aga Khan Cultural Services – Egypt and the ALIPH Foundation, at a cost of more than 100 million Egyptian pounds. The work will take eight months and is due to be completed in May 2027, in cooperation with the Cairo Governorate and the Ministries of Antiquities and Endowments.\n\nThe project aims to protect the architectural and historical value of the market, to preserve one of Cairo's most important centres of traditional handicrafts, to revive its cultural and economic role, and to make sure this heritage is passed on to future generations.\n\nThe Governor was accompanied on his tour by Dr Ahmed Anwar Atiyya Al-Adl, Deputy Governor of Cairo for the Western Zone; Sherif Arian, Chief Executive Officer of the Aga Khan Cultural Services – Egypt; Major General Ahmed Helmy, Director of Government Relations at the organisation; Walid Hegazy, Director of Projects and Asset Management at the Aga Khan organisation; Ashraf Boutros, consultant for the restoration of Qasabat Radwan and Al-Khayamiya; and a number of Governorate officials.\n\nThe Governor stressed that the project reflects the importance of treating heritage as a living asset that contributes to development, not merely a witness to the past. It combines the conservation of historic buildings with an improved urban environment and support for traditional activities, strengthening residents' bond with their cultural inheritance and boosting cultural tourism in Al-Darb Al-Ahmar.\n\nHe added that the work in the Khayamiya area is part of the effort to preserve the visual identity of historic Cairo and to strike a balance between safeguarding the authenticity of heritage sites and the needs of contemporary life. This reinforces the market's standing as a destination for handicrafts and folk heritage, and ensures that the colours and patterns of the Khayamiya remain part of Cairo's character and historical memory.\n\nThe Governor confirmed that the work has been coordinated in advance with the traders and shop owners in the area, so that business can continue and shops will not be closed during the restoration. This protects the traders' interests and livelihoods while safeguarding the historical and archaeological value of the place.\n\nHe also praised the role of the Aga Khan organisation in partnering with the Egyptian state to preserve and revive heritage, pointing to the importance of cooperating with international institutions concerned with heritage conservation on projects that revive historic areas while keeping their character and traditional activities.\n\nThe first phase of the restoration includes upgrading the shops, walls and floors; improving the lighting network; restoring the woodwork and mashrabiya screens; and repairing the market's wooden roof, one of the monument's most distinctive architectural features. Qasabat Radwan Bey is one of the few historic markets that still has its roof, and its restoration helps preserve the market's characteristic architecture.\n\nSouq Al-Khayamiya lies outside the historic gate of Bab Zuweila, behind the Mosque of Al-Salih Tala'i, and overlooks Al-Khayamiya Street in Al-Darb Al-Ahmar. The street is historically linked to the making, decorating and selling of tents and is one of the most important heritage craft areas in Cairo. The site is known as Qasabat Radwan Bey and is associated with commercial and urban structures from the Ottoman period and the 17th century. The qasaba was built in 1062 AH (1652 CE) and was registered as a monument in 1951.\n\nThe site is associated with Prince Radwan ibn Abdullah Al-Faqari, one of the leading Faqari emirs of the Ottoman era, who served as Emir of the Sultanic Banner in Egypt for two years and then as Emir of the Hajj for eight years. He lived through the reigns of Sultans Murad IV, Ibrahim I and Mehmed IV, and a number of Ottoman governors of Egypt. He died in 1066 AH (1655 CE).\n\nThe market's architecture reflects the nature of trade and daily life in Cairo through the ages. The monument combines varied architectural elements: a main entrance leading to a covered passage, open courtyards, stone storehouses and an architectural bench, along with storeys built of red brick and decorated wooden windows.\n\nThe covered roof is one of the monument's most notable features. It is made of wooden planks carried on beams that span its width, with rectangular openings that let in light and air. The area also has two prayer rooms, one eastern and one western, serving the traders and workers.\n\nThe market is tied to the craft of the Khayamiya, an authentic Egyptian craft based on cutting coloured fabrics and stitching them by hand into geometric and artistic patterns. Over generations it has developed from the making of tents and pavilions into a heritage art that expresses Egyptian identity. It is linked to social and religious occasions and to Islamic ornament, and its products are used to decorate homes and streets, especially during the month of Ramadan.\n\nToday the market's historic storehouses are used as workshops where Khayamiya is made and decorated and its products sold. This makes the site a living space that combines historic architecture with economic and craft activity, preserves inherited expertise and the craftspeople's livelihoods, and supports the passing of the craft on to new generations.",
